Chapter: RealLiteralBoundary

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 The passing side of CDX2073, and it is the half that makes the refusal
 worth having. Nineteen accumulated digits is the largest a Real literal
 can carry, because `__text_to_double` folds every digit on both sides of
 the point into one i64 before scaling, and i64's maximum is itself
 nineteen digits. So nineteen needs a COMPARISON against 9223372036854775807
 rather than a length test, and a guard that refused at nineteen would
 refuse legitimate values.

 The digits are counted across the point, not within the integer part:
 `1.123456789012345678` and `123456789012345678.0` both accumulate the same
 nineteen digits and both must compile. Steve Howell asked for the boundary
 to be covered from both sides; its other side is
 codex/test/errors/real-literal-overflow.

 Both rows report through `real-to-int` and NOT through `show`, deliberately.
 Measured 2026-09-01 while capturing this file's sidecar: `show` on a Real
 corrupts a digit once the integer part passes sixteen, printing
 `123456789012345678.0` as `103456789012345680.0` and
 `12345678901234567.0` as `02345678901234566.0`, while `real-to-int` on the
 same values is correct. That is a printer defect and not this row's, so
 asserting it here would have recorded a wrong answer as gold.

Section: Definitions

  frac-nineteen : Real = 1.123456789012345678

  whole-nineteen : Real = 123456789012345678.0

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("whole-19-parses : " & show (real-to-int whole-nineteen))
    print-line-uni ("frac-19-parses  : " & show (real-to-int (frac-nineteen + frac-nineteen)))
  end

